<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Shakespeare is a 51-year-old man with essential tremor who started propranolol extended-release 60 mg one week ago and came in the same day with a cold. Two days ago he developed a sore throat and a blocked, running nose, then a dry cough; last night his temperature was 37.9 C. Several people at a reading he attended last week have had colds. He is eating and drinking normally. He has no shortness of breath, wheeze, chest pain, ear or facial pain, or rash. He bought a day-and-night cold remedy containing a decongestant and a night-time syrup with an antihistamine and alcohol, and asked whether he could take them with propranolol; he has not started them. On propranolol his hand is a little steadier, and he has had no dizziness, wheeze, or unusual tiredness. He has kept to about two drinks on most evenings and had none since the cold began. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Onset</td><td>Two days; sore throat first, then runny nose and cough</td></tr><tr><td>Associated signs and symptoms</td><td>Low-grade fever (37.9 C), dry cough, aches</td></tr><tr><td>Pertinent negatives</td><td>No breathlessness, wheeze, chest pain, ear or sinus pain, or rash</td></tr><tr><td>Medicines</td><td>Propranolol extended-release 60 mg for one week; cold remedies bought but not taken</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

