    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Acute viral upper respiratory infection (J06.9)</content>. A common cold with a low-grade fever after exposure to people with colds. No features of streptococcal pharyngitis, sinusitis, or pneumonia, and no wheeze on a nonselective beta-blocker. He is 51 with no chronic heart or lung disease, so testing would not change treatment; antibiotics do not help a cold. [S]<list><item>No antibiotic and no testing.</item><item>Acetaminophen 500 mg to 1 g every 6 hours as needed for fever or aches, no more than 3 g a day.</item><item>Do not take the decongestant (pseudoephedrine or phenylephrine) cold remedies: they raise blood pressure, can make tremor worse, and work against propranolol; use saline spray and steam instead.</item><item>Do not take the night-time syrup, which contains alcohol and a sedating antihistamine; honey and warm drinks for the cough.</item><item>No alcohol while unwell.</item><item>Return for breathlessness, wheeze, chest pain, fever above 38.5 C after three days, or symptoms beyond ten days.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Essential tremor (G25.0)</content>. One week of propranolol 60 mg with an early, small improvement; pulse 70 even with the fever; no side effects. [S]<list><item>Continue propranolol 60 mg each morning; do not stop it suddenly.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Hazardous drinking (Z72.89)</content>. Keeping to about two drinks most evenings since the brief intervention; none since the cold began. [S]<list><item>Continue the agreed limits.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Evidence-based guideline update: treatment of essential tremor: report of the Quality Standards Subcommittee of the American Academy of Neurology (2011; reaffirmed 2025) (2011); Appropriate Antibiotic Use for Acute Respiratory Tract Infection in Adults: Advice for High-Value Care From the American College of Physicians and the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (2016)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Preventive visit in two weeks as booked; sooner for the warning signs above. Next scheduled visit 1616-02-16: preventive health visit at 51. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
